WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
well this week saw Boris Johnson's 8th  parliamentary defeat as Prime Minister  and David Gorkhas one of the 21 former  Conservative MP stripped to the whip for  voting against him and the former  justice secretary is starting to make a  bit of a habit of rebelling he joins us  now from his constituency in  hartfordshire thank you for being with  us and first of all we're here at  concept in party confidence you voted  against allowing a short recess in order  for it to go ahead is that not a bit an  insult to the activists who've spent a  lot of money coming here no because it's  perfectly possible to carry on with  conference and that's exactly what's  happening and conferences carrying on  ministers will be there I will be going  to conference this afternoon so the two  can carry on but I think it is important  given that Parliament hadn't been  sitting for a number of days as it  turned out unlawfully that we make use  of the time available that Parliament is  able to carry on and do its work so I  think it's right that both the  conference goes ahead and that  Parliament continues to sit and now you  said that Boris Johnson is borrowing  from in your words the Donald Trump  playbook are you worried about the  direction that your party is going in  yes I am worried about the direction the  conservative party is going in at the  moment I think there is a choice for us  we can return to our traditions of being  a broad church a party that is  responsible that is competent that looks  to unite the country as much as possible  or we can take an approach whereby we  seek to divide the country that we call  sin our language that we only seek to  appeal to a particular part of society  and we demonize the rest that we  undermine our institutions and there are  aspects of what has been happening in  recent weeks that I fear fall into that  second category and it does remind me of  the way that Donald Trump is now running  the Republican Party in the u.s. I think  that is a a disappointing state of  affairs to put it mildly I don't believe  that's right for the Conservative Party  I don't believe it's right for the  country and I hope that we see a change  of direction in the course of the next  few days at the Conservative Party  conference  I think it's interesting that trumpian  is almost used as an insult there are a  lot of people obviously in America who  voted for Donald Trump is it's not just  a bit of a pejorative way of talking  about people whose opinions you don't  really like well look I think the  conservative party should aim higher  than the standards of Donald Trump that  the approach that he takes to politics  the approach that he takes to  truth-telling  the approach that he takes in terms of  dividing his country is not something  that I want to see in the United Kingdom  and it's certainly not something that I  want to see from the party I've been a  member of for 29 years so I I make no  apology for criticizing the Donald Trump  style of politics it's not what I want  to see from the Conservative Party and I  think never mind bad for the country  that's really important but if that's  the approach that we go for the  Conservative Party will be waving  goodbye to millions of its long-standing  supporters and at a time when MPs are  concerned for their safety there's been  a lot of focus in Parliament this week  about the language and that our  politicians are using in particular of  course number 10 and also the prime  minister talking about the surrender act  talking about humiliation saying we will  not betray the people are you worried  about some of that language and do you  think that in the words of amber Raddatz  inciting violence I am worried about the  use of language at the moment it is  incredibly divisive it is designed to  stoke resentment to provoke anger to  nurse grievances and it is feeding into  a toxic toxic atmosphere not just in  Parliament but I think more widely I  think we have to recognize that people  can take different views on the issue of  brexit but hold those views in good  faith and I think this applies to all  sides because I think there's you know  one side has had a tendency to sneer the  other side has had a tendency to  denigrate and accuse people of being  unpatriotic  got to recognize that people of good  faith and intelligence can hold  different views on this matter and that  ultimately as a country we've got to  come back together again at some point  we heard from at Hancock earlier the  health secretary who was defending the  use of the word those surrender acting  said it's actually is you know an  accurate way of describing what the ban  acts thinks to do when it is of course  when we talk about these things and just  for the viewers you know it's it's the  the idea that MPs voted to compel the  Prime Minister to seek an extension  rather than leave with no deal if you  can't get a deal with the EU now there's  a story in the mail on sunday today  saying that people involved in the  drafting of that act got help from  Brussels you were very close to what  happened with that act is that true no  it's not true  and I think here is a very good example  of a number-10 briefing using the word  collusion I mean that's a very loaded  word in itself collusion providing no  evidence that there was anything  supporting this statement that there was  help with the drafting coming from  foreign countries it's not true but even  if it were true the use of language of  that sort is completely disproportionate  completely over the top and feeds into  this narrative there anyone who doesn't  agree with number Ten's position is  somehow unpatriotic or betraying the  country or an enemy or wanting the  country to surrender I mean this is the  sort of language that I think a lot of  people are concerned about because it is  questioning motives of people who are  just trying to do the best for this  country and avoid what most people I  think who've studied this closely would  accept would be a disastrous outcome of  us leaving without a deal on the 31st of  October causing great damage to our  prosperity and the integrity of the  United Kingdom is it right that there  are plans to try and look at a second  piece of legislation that would  effectively allow the speaker to bypass  the prime minister to request an  extension if necessary  well I don't wanna get drawn into  specific and ideas but what I would say  is that the Ben Act is effective it does  what it intends to do and it should be  abided by and I very much hope that the  government will be clear that they will  abide by the law and you know there are  lots of suggestions of loopholes and so  on as far as I can see anything that's  being suggested so far it can be easily  dealt with and if necessary the courts  will ensure that the will of Parliament  and the law is complied with it's  interesting you say that mean what do  you think the consequences would be if  Downing Street if the Prime Minister did  try to ignore the Ben Act well in truth  if there's an attempt to not follow the  law then the law will take its course  and it'll be a question of going to the  courts and the court will ensure that  the law is complied with but we are a  country that is governed by laws that is  our history that is our tradition is one  of the most important things about the  United Kingdom and the Prime Minister to  be fair has been clear that he's going  to abide by the law but to suggest  otherwise these sort of hints that all  somehow we're not going to do that I  think is is dangerous it undermines the  rule of law in this country and that's  not the role of government to do far  from it and then just finally you of  course have had the Conservative whip  withdrawn for voting against the  government and previously I'm interested  to know what your next steps are what  your plans are do you want to be  readmitted to the Conservative Party you  want to run as an independent where are  you looking at what what are your plans  what's your thoughts well look I  consider myself a conservative I've been  a member of the Conservative Party for  29 years the first time I rebelled  against the conservative whip after 14  years as an MP I had the whip withdrawn  I would like to fight the next general  election in my constituency southwest  half for chair as the Conservative  candidate but it's obviously not  entirely within my control if I can't do  that  obviously consider my options but as far  as I'm concerned I consider myself a  conservative I think my views are  consistent with the best traditions of  the Conservative Party and I think  represent the views of many conservative  voters  okay David Gork thank you very much for  being on the program today thank you

